; COBRA 2 -- the Cobra Mk III flying straight, a camera circling it, and
; the sky, in CoolAction!
;
; **COBRA is the ship; this is the view** (D106). The same ship and the
; same arithmetic, every frame computed between two vertical blanks
; (D105), read the other way round: the ship does not turn, the camera
; circles it at half COBRA's rates, and the ship flies straight along its
; length. The ship's frame is then the world's, so the rotation COBRA
; computes is the camera's, and everything fixed in the world goes
; through it: the ship's corners, the stars, the dust.
;
; **The sky is Elite's, placed in the world.** A star is a direction, so
; the rotation alone moves it, and it swings across the screen exactly
; as far as the camera turns. A speck of dust is a point fixed in the
; world, and the ship flies through it: the ship's place (sh) moves SPEED
; a frame along its nose, the camera follows, and what goes through the
; rotation and the perspective, as a corner does, is the speck's place
; from the ship -- its world place less the ship's. Elite keeps a handful
; of stardust and recycles each speck that leaves the screen; so does
; this. A star re-enters across the edge opposite the one it left by, a
; speck somewhere in view ahead of the ship, so it streams past it, and
; each is carried back into the world through the matrix's transpose,
; where it stays fixed until it leaves again.
;
; **Behind the ship, the sky is hidden.** The ship is lines, so a star
; behind it would show through its body, and filling it black would cost
; a frame. Its outline is the edges where a face turned to the camera
; meets one turned away (Visible()), and a point between that outline's
; crossings of its row is not drawn -- a star always, a speck when it is
; behind the ship's centre. It stays in the sky, and comes out again past
; the ship's edge.
;
; **The screen is a mode of its own**: mode 6's full height, 256 x 240,
; at 4 bits a pixel -- 30,720 bytes a page, so two pages fit VRAM, at
; $0000 and $7800, and the display flips between them (D92). Mode 5 is
; the same depth and gives 48 of the 240 lines to borders; mode 6 at 8
; bits is 61,440 bytes a page and cannot double-buffer at all. The pixel
; port writes a 4-bit pixel in one store and steps X or Y itself, which
; is the fastest thing on the machine for a line.
;
; **The flip goes before the wait, not after it.** The fetch takes
; VID_DBASE at the frame start, and the frame counter WaitVBlank watches
; ticks on the same pulse (cool8_vregs.v), so a page flipped after
; WaitVBlank() reaches the glass a frame late -- and the page drawn on
; next is the one on show, erased and redrawn in full view. Flipped when
; it is finished, a page is taken at the next frame start, and only then
; is the other one drawn on.
;
; **Where the clocks are spent in assembly**: Transform (a multiply-add
; is 28 clocks and has no branch, because every signed value carries 128
; into the unsigned MUL and the surplus comes back off as two constants),
; Faces (the same multiply-add, over the normals), Sky (the same rows
; over every star and speck, and a perspective that is a table and a
; MUL), Lines (a pixel is one store and an error step, 14 clocks, and 23
; on a pixel where the other axis steps) and Dots. The rest -- the
; rotation's fifteen products, the edge list, putting points back into
; view -- is compiled.
;
; Runs until a key is pressed.

